You are diagnosing and fixing a real soundness bug in `verus`, an SMT-based verifier for Rust (it proves Rust programs satisfy their specifications). The bug is in verus's own source code. You work ONLY inside this repository checkout:

    REPO ROOT: /Users/junekim/Documents/verus-p11   (currently at the base commit)

## The bug
verus-lang/verus issue #2219: "ghost uses of never type invalidates borrowchecking"

Verus WRONGLY ACCEPTS (verifies) this program, which it should REJECT — `t` is a `tracked` (linear/affine) resource and is consumed twice:

```rust
proof fn test(nvr: !) { }
proof fn consume<T>(tracked t: T) { }
uninterp spec fn arbitrary<T>() -> T;

proof fn test2<T>(tracked t: T) {
    test(arbitrary());

    consume(t);
    consume(t);
}
```

The ghost use of the `!`-typed value causes Rust to treat the code after it as unreachable, so verus's borrow/linearity check for `tracked` resources is silently skipped and the double-consume slips through. A correct verifier must report an error here (e.g. "use of moved value: `t`").

## What counts as DONE (read carefully — a NARROW fix is NOT acceptable)
Making the verifier reject the reported program is necessary but NOT sufficient. The reported issue is ONE example. A fix keyed to incidental features of how that example happens to be written — so that the reported program is rejected but OTHER programs that are unsound for the same underlying reason are still wrongly accepted — is an OVER-NARROW fix and will be REJECTED. Deliver the MOST GENERAL fix that stays SOUND and does not over-reject:
- generality: every program unsound for the same root reason is now rejected, not just the reported one;
- no over-rejection: programs that are genuinely valid still verify.
Before declaring done, attack your own fix as an adversary would: construct programs that differ from the reported one ONLY in incidental ways and confirm the unsound ones are now rejected, and construct a genuinely-valid variant and confirm it still verifies. Do NOT stop at the first fix that rejects the reported program. Stop only when you cannot construct any unsound program your fix wrongly accepts, nor any valid one it wrongly rejects.

## How to run verus on a single test program
verus verifies one `.rs` file at a time. From the repo root:

    cd /Users/junekim/Documents/verus-p11/source && env -u RUSTC -u RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN PATH=/tmp/rustup-shims:$PATH \
      bash -c 'vargo build --release >/dev/null 2>&1 && ./target-verus/release/verus --crate-type=lib <path-to-file.rs>'

Interpreting output:
- a line like `verification results:: N verified, M errors` with `M > 0`, OR any `error[...]` / "use of moved value" => the program was REJECTED.
- `N verified, 0 errors` with no error lines => the program VERIFIED (was accepted).
Proof/spec code goes inside `verus! { ... }`; a file needs `fn main() {}`. Files using `!` need `#![allow(internal_features)] #![feature(never_type)]` at the top. IMPORTANT: that command REBUILDS the verifier (`vargo build --release`) before running, so it always reflects your latest source edits. A bare `./target-verus/release/verus` does NOT pick up edits — always use the command above.

## Where the verifier lives
verus's own source is under `source/` — especially `source/rust_verify/src/` (mode/erasure and VIR generation: `erase.rs`, `rust_to_vir_expr.rs`, `fn_call_to_vir.rs`) and the vendored MIR-builder fork under `source/rustc_mir_build/` and `source/rustc_mir_build_additional_files/` (where Rust's control-flow graph is constructed and unreachable code is pruned). The bug's thread: the ghost `!`-typed expression makes Rust delete the control-flow edge after it, so the verifier never checks the following statements.

